      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "BLK_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_87EB50DB_CONCEPTS_01",
          "semanticAnchorId": "ANCHOR__87EB50DB_1",
          "title": "1. Key Academic Principles: Power, Elastic and Inelastic Collisions in One and Two Dimensions",
          "contentMarkdown": "Power is defined as the time rate of doing work or transferring energy: P = dW/dt = F . v. The SI unit of power is the watt (W = J/s), where 1 horsepower equals 746 watts. In all physical collisions, total linear momentum is strictly conserved because external forces are negligible during collision. In an elastic collision, both total linear momentum and total kinetic energy are conserved with coefficient of restitution e = 1.",
          "boundAtomicFactIds": [
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_87EB50DB_670AC602",
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_87EB50DB_1F4ED078",
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_87EB50DB_AE20ED79",
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_87EB50DB_C717A04D"
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "BLK_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_87EB50DB_CONCEPTS_02",
          "semanticAnchorId": "ANCHOR__87EB50DB_2",
          "title": "2. Key Academic Principles: Power, Elastic and Inelastic Collisions in One and Two Dimensions",
          "contentMarkdown": "In an inelastic collision, total linear momentum is conserved while total kinetic energy is not conserved, with 0 <= e < 1. In a completely inelastic collision, the interacting bodies stick together after impact and move with common final velocity. Instantaneous power is the limiting value of average power as time interval approaches zero: P = dW/dt = F . Another practical unit is horsepower: 1 hp = 746 W.",
          "boundAtomicFactIds": [
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_87EB50DB_66B655EC",
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_87EB50DB_4F62FAAD",
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_87EB50DB_90F382BB",
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_87EB50DB_3C1CC63F"
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "BLK_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_87EB50DB_CONCEPTS_03",
          "semanticAnchorId": "ANCHOR__87EB50DB_3",
          "title": "3. Key Academic Principles: Power, Elastic and Inelastic Collisions in One and Two Dimensions",
          "contentMarkdown": "In a 1D elastic collision between two masses m1 and m2, final velocities are: v1 = ((m1 - m2)/(m1 + m2))*u1 + (2*m2/(m1 + m2))*u2, and v2 = (2*m1/(m1 + m2))*u1 + ((m2 - m1)/(m1 + m2))*u2. In a 1D elastic collision, final velocities are v1 = ((m1 - m2)/(m1 + m2))*u1 + (2*m2/(m1 + m2))*u2, and v2 = (2*m1/(m1 + m2))*u1 + ((m2 - m1)/(m1 + m2))*u2.",
          "boundAtomicFactIds": [
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_87EB50DB_2EABCD26",
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_87EB50DB_C1DC0D68",
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_87EB50DB_E536B647"
          ]
        }
      ],
